From e224bae45068d36c23bce9228ba8d91b34215982 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 01:46:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] minicargo: accept `-j<count>` with the count attached

Only `-j <count>` was read. For `-j2` the option code looked at the next
argument, saw it start with `-`, and took it as a bare `-j`, so the
count was dropped and minicargo built one crate at a time. MacPorts'
cargo PortGroup passes `-j${build.jobs}`, so every port built serially
even on multi-CPU machines ("1r" with dozens of ready crates waiting).

With three independent crates under one root, `-j2` now starts the
second while the first is still running ("2r"), like `-j 2`; `-j1` and
no `-j` still build one at a time.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 tools/minicargo/main.cpp | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/tools/minicargo/main.cpp b/tools/minicargo/main.cpp
index 50e68eb5..85fd41e0 100644
--- a/tools/minicargo/main.cpp
+++ b/tools/minicargo/main.cpp
@@ -540,6 +540,11 @@ int ProgramOptions::parse(int argc, const char* argv[])
                 this->output_directory = argv[++i];
                 break;
             case 'j':
+                // `-j<count>`, as cargo accepts it (MacPorts' cargo PortGroup passes `-j${build.jobs}`)
+                if( arg[2] != '\0' ) {
+                    this->build_jobs = ::std::strtol(arg + 2, nullptr, 10);
+                    break;
+                }
                 if( i+1 == argc || argv[i+1][0] == '-' ) {
                     // TODO: Detect number of CPU cores and use that many jobs
                     break;
